|tips =
*Face toward a tight crowd of enemies before placing a turret, to best make use of the stacking damage bonus.
*Because the turret cannot turn (without the augment), face it in a direction where enemies already exist, or are likely to soon appear.
*When heading to extraction, a turret deployed facing ahead will both fire at enemies in front, as well as those who come up from behind.
*Since the damage multiplier is based on the number of enemies hit by each plasma stream, Blaze Artillery's damage rises ''very'' quickly when used in crowded hallways and tight corridors, where plasma streams can pierce through multiple enemies aligned with the shot.
**{{Weapon|Scyotid}}'s alt-fire, {{WF|Nidus}}'s {{A|Larva}} (subsumable), {{Companion|Nautilus}}'s {{M|Cordon}}, and slide attacks with {{Weapon|Telos Boltace}} can force enemies into tighter groups, to help maximize Blaze Artillery's hits per shot.
**One can also arrange parallel lines of Shrapnel Vortexes with {{A|Grenade Fan}}, creating a funnel to push enemies into a single lane.
*Artillery turrets prefer to target the closest enemy first, then switch between targets that are within range. Use this behavior to your advantage to spread the damage around and apply {{D|Heat}} status effects to stun multiple enemies.
*Due to the plasma streams only punching infinitely through ''bodies'', it's better to deploy Blaze Artillery near the ground on the same level as the enemies it faces. If placed high up, the shots will impact the ground prematurely.
**However, if there are obstacles to contend with, deploying a turret from higher up can give it a greater field of view.
*Activate {{A|Temporal Anchor}} and place multiple turrets. Direct damage from plasma turrets, including the damage over time from {{D|Heat}} status effects, are recorded to fuel the temporal implosion.
**Energy spent on the turrets is returned once Protea rewinds.
**Alternatively, use the temporal implosion to pull enemies toward a central location, then deploy turrets to increase their damage multipliers quickly.
*The ever-increasing damage multiplier has several interesting properties:
**For instance, it causes [[Ability Strength]] to have diminishing returns on damage. At 100% Ability Strength, Blaze Artillery will kill an enemy with 5000 health in four hits: 500, then 1000, then 1500, then 2000, for 5000 total damage (ignoring the heat status effect for simplicity). Increasing Ability Strength to 167% will kill the same enemy in three shots (835, then 1670, then 2505), which is a DPS increase of only 33%.
**By the same token, placing three Blaze Artillery turrets in the same direction does NOT triple your average DPS compared to a single one. Using an example target with 15000 health, the three turrets will kill it in 4 shots each (1500, then 3000, then 4500, then 6000), while the singular turret will need 8 shots (i.e. 3 turrets together kill only twice as fast as a lone turret). Taking the heat status effect into account makes this effect even more pronounced.
**This applies to every "damage increasing effect" in one way or another, such as using Viral status, {{A|Roar}}, shredding armor, Heat Inherit, etc. They remain valuable, but not as much.
**This also applies the other way around: an enemy having twice as much health won't take twice as much time to kill. It also means that high-armor targets like Grineer won't have a disproportionately higher time-to-kill compared to other factions, even without {{M|Temporal Erosion}}.
**In general, this makes Ability Duration more valuable than Ability Strength for scaling the damage of individual turrets. It also means that it's usually better to cover non-overlapping areas when deploying multiple turrets, rather than stacking them in the same place.


